maya: Heads up — the JS quickstart in README uses top-level await + ESM `import`. That trips folks on CommonJS projects and older Node LTS (Windows users reported `SyntaxError: Unexpected identifier`). Current snippet we have:
```js
import Redwood from "redwood-inference";
const client = new Redwood({ apiKey: process.env.REDWOOD_API_KEY });
const resp = await client.generate("hello");
console.log(resp.text);
```
This is causing a bunch of copy/paste confusion in onboarding tickets.

dom: Oof, saw that in support too. Two quick fixes: (1) add a note that the snippet requires Node >= 14 with ESM or use `type: "module"`, and (2) provide a CommonJS `require` variant. Example CommonJS wrapper:
```js
const Redwood = require('redwood-inference');
(async () => {
  const client = new Redwood({ apiKey: process.env.REDWOOD_API_KEY });
  const resp = await client.generate('hi');
  console.log(resp.text);
})();
```

maya: Also add a Windows env var hint — folks on PowerShell/cmd keep pasting `export REDWOOD_API_KEY=...` which doesn't work. Quick examples to add: PowerShell: `$env:REDWOOD_API_KEY="XXX"`  cmd: `set REDWOOD_API_KEY=XXX`  and maybe mention `setx` for persistent.

sam: Can we avoid top-level await in the main example and instead show an `async function main()` wrapper? Less explaining for beginners. Eg:
```js
import Redwood from 'redwood-inference';
async function main() {
  const client = new Redwood({ apiKey: process.env.REDWOOD_API_KEY });
  const resp = await client.generate('hello');
  console.log(resp.text);
}
main().catch(console.error);
```

oliver: I'll open a docs PR with both the ESM note, CommonJS snippet, and Windows env var short table. Also add a tiny CI smoke test that runs the quickstart script in Node 16/18. Opening PR: https://github.com/redwood-inference/docs/pull/412 :thumbsup:

maya: Awesome — can you also add a short TS example (with `ts-node` caveat) and call out Node 14 vs 16 differences? A couple of recent onboarding tickets were TS users.

